TASK

Produce 3 to 5 COMPETING hypotheses for what is really costing this business money or time.

Rules that decide whether this is worth anything:

1. A hypothesis is a CAUSE, never a solution. "They lose evening enquiries because nobody answers after 6pm" is a cause. "They need an AI receptionist" is a solution — that is not your job and it is not a hypothesis.

2. They must genuinely COMPETE. If all of your hypotheses could be true at once, you have written one hypothesis five times. Each should point at a different part of the business: demand, conversion, capacity, delivery, cost, pricing, retention.

3. At least one must NOT be solvable by software. Real businesses fail on pricing, on hiring, on the owner's time, on a bad location, on a contract they signed. A diagnosis that can only ever conclude "build software" is not a diagnosis.

4. Do not simply restate what the owner told you. The owner's stated problem is a hypothesis like any other — include it, label it honestly, and let it be tested. Owners are often right about the symptom and wrong about the cause.

5. For each one, state what evidence WOULD support it and what evidence WOULD refute it. Cite claim ids like CF-01 where their own numbers are relevant. If no number they gave bears on it, say so — an honest "nothing they gave tests this" is worth more than a fabricated link.

6. Never invent a number. Every figure you mention must come from THEIR OWN NUMBERS above, cited by id.

Respond with ONLY this JSON shape, no markdown fences, no commentary:

